const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Australian Raven live?', a: 'The Australian Raven (Corvus coronoides) is found in south-eastern and south-western Australia — essentially the temperate and semi-arid zones of the continent. Its range covers most of New South Wales, Victoria, South Australia, and the south-west corner of Western Australia, with extensions into southern Queensland and southern Northern Territory. It is the dominant large corvid of the coastal regions, cities, and agricultural zones of south-eastern Australia.' },
  { q: 'Is the Australian Raven found in all of Australia?', a: 'No. The Australian Raven is absent from tropical northern Australia (which is dominated by the Torresian Crow), from most of central Australia (which has the Little Crow and Torresian Crow), and from Tasmania (where it is replaced by the Forest Raven). The five Australian corvid species each occupy different parts of the continent, with limited but real overlap zones where identification can be challenging.' },
  { q: 'How does the Australian Raven differ from the other Australian corvids in distribution?', a: 'Australia has five corvid species: Australian Raven (south-east and south-west), Little Raven (south-east, alpine and coastal), Forest Raven (Tasmania, south-east coastal), Torresian Crow (north and centre), and Little Crow (arid interior). The Australian Raven is the largest, most territorial, and most strongly associated with tall trees and urban areas in the south-east. It overlaps with the Little Raven in south-eastern Australia, where the two can be confused in the field.' },
];
