    <h1>Australian Raven Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven (<em>Corvus coronoides</em>) is found across south-eastern and south-western Australia — from southern Queensland south through New South Wales, Victoria, South Australia, and the south-west of Western Australia. It is the dominant large corvid of coastal and agricultural south-eastern Australia. It is absent from Tasmania (Forest Raven), tropical northern Australia (Torresian Crow), and most of the arid interior.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven occupies temperate and semi-arid zones in the southern half of Australia:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>New South Wales:</strong> throughout — the most common large corvid in both rural and urban NSW. Absent only from the highest alpine areas (where Little Raven is more common) and far north-western desert.</li>
      <li><strong>Victoria:</strong> throughout — common in Melbourne and other cities, in farmland, and in open forest.</li>
      <li><strong>South Australia:</strong> throughout settled and semi-arid areas; extends into drier country than other ravens.</li>
      <li><strong>Western Australia (south-west):</strong> confined to the south-western corner of WA — the high-rainfall area around Perth and the karri/jarrah forests south of Perth. Absent from the arid interior of WA.</li>
      <li><strong>Southern Queensland:</strong> extends into south-east Queensland, becoming less common northward.</li>
      <li><strong>Northern Territory (south):</strong> extends marginally into the southern NT in drier shrubland.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The Australian Raven is absent from Tasmania (Forest Raven territory), tropical Queensland and the Northern Territory (Torresian Crow), and the deep arid centre.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Australian Raven occupies a wide range of open and semi-open habitats:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary:</strong> open woodland with tall trees (eucalypts), agricultural land, pastoral land, and open forest. Strongly associated with tall trees for nesting.</li>
      <li><strong>Urban:</strong> common in parks, suburbs, and city centres of south-eastern Australian cities — Sydney, Melbourne, Canberra, Adelaide. One of the most familiar urban birds in Australia.</li>
      <li><strong>Semi-arid:</strong> extends into mallee and mulga scrubland, using scattered tall trees or pylons for nesting.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoided:</strong> dense closed-canopy rainforest, tropical woodland, and the deep arid interior without access to water.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

